### Summary

If a workspace folder is removed while the Git extension is still opening its repository, the repository ends up open anyway. It stays in the Source Control view and in `api.repositories` even though no workspace folder contains it. In our runs it was still there after 60 s.

This happens when an extension replaces a workspace folder quickly (for example `updateWorkspaceFolders(1, 1, { uri })` every few hundred ms). It is timing dependent, so slower machines (slow disk, antivirus scanning `.git`, large repositories) should hit it more often.

### Where it comes from

In [`extensions/git/src/model.ts`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/ca425775e4227ef254db9dadbbcaca943f40cda1/extensions/git/src/model.ts):

- [`onDidChangeWorkspaceFolders`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/ca425775e4227ef254db9dadbbcaca943f40cda1/extensions/git/src/model.ts#L470-L493) disposes only repositories that are **already open** for the removed folders, then awaits `openRepository` for the added ones.
- [`openRepository`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/ca425775e4227ef254db9dadbbcaca943f40cda1/extensions/git/src/model.ts#L600-L690) awaits several steps (`getRepositoryRoot`, `isRepositoryOutsideWorkspace`, `getRepositoryDotGit` with the real path, `workspace.requestResourceTrust`) before [`this.open(repository)`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/ca425775e4227ef254db9dadbbcaca943f40cda1/extensions/git/src/model.ts#L690).
- Nothing checks, after the last await, that the path is still inside a workspace folder. So if folder A is added and then removed before its `openRepository` finishes, the removal finds nothing to dispose, and the open then completes and registers A.

### Evidence

In an isolated VS Code (fresh `--user-data-dir`/`--extensions-dir`, `--disable-extensions`, built-in Git only), a test extension swapped folder #1 between two Git repositories B → A → B, waiting for each `onDidChangeWorkspaceFolders` before the next swap. Times are ms from the first swap:

- Gap 200 ms: swaps fired at 25 (B), 257 (A) and 520 (B). `Git.log` shows `[Model][openRepository] Opened repository` for **A at 548**, 28 ms after A's folder had been removed. A stayed in Source Control for the rest of the 60 s poll.
- Gap 500 ms, two runs: `Opened repository` for A came **9 ms** and **15 ms** after the swap that removed A. Both times A remained orphaned.

Gaps of 0 ms, 1500 ms and 5000 ms never produced an orphan. With 200–500 ms gaps it reproduced in 3 of 5 runs in one batch and 0 of about 37 later runs on the same machine, so it is **intermittent**. The rate looks load dependent.

In real use this shows up with an extension that makes the workspace follow the focused terminal pane. Switching panes between two repositories about once a second left the previous repository in Source Control until some later rescan.

### Repro

<details><summary>Minimal extension (intermittent; may need many cycles or a loaded machine)</summary>

`package.json`
```json
{
  "name": "git-orphan-repro",
  "version": "0.0.1",
  "engines": { "vscode": "^1.90.0" },
  "main": "./extension.js",
  "activationEvents": ["onStartupFinished"]
}
```

`extension.js` (open a multi-root workspace `[<any non-git folder>, /path/to/repoA]` first)
```js
const vscode = require('vscode');
const path = require('path');

const A = vscode.Uri.file('/path/to/repoA'); // git repo
const B = vscode.Uri.file('/path/to/repoB'); // another git repo
const GAP_MS = 500;
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
const inside = (p, root) => path.relative(root, p) === '' || !path.relative(root, p).startsWith('..');

// Replace workspace folder #1 and wait for the change event (never overlap two updates).
const swap = (uri) => new Promise((resolve) => {
  const d = vscode.workspace.onDidChangeWorkspaceFolders(() => { d.dispose(); resolve(); });
  vscode.workspace.updateWorkspaceFolders(1, 1, { uri });
});

exports.activate = async () => {
  const api = (await vscode.extensions.getExtension('vscode.git').activate()).getAPI(1);
  const orphans = () => api.repositories.map((r) => r.rootUri.fsPath)
    .filter((r) => !(vscode.workspace.workspaceFolders || []).some((f) => inside(r, f.uri.fsPath)));
  for (let i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
    await swap(B); await sleep(GAP_MS);
    await swap(A); await sleep(GAP_MS);
    await swap(B);
    await sleep(5000); // let everything settle
    const o = orphans();
    if (o.length) {
      vscode.window.showErrorMessage(`Cycle ${i}: orphan repository in Source Control: ${o.join(', ')}`);
      return;
    }
    await swap(A); await sleep(5000);
  }
  vscode.window.showInformationMessage('No orphan repository after 20 cycles');
};
```
</details>

**Expected:** after the last swap, Source Control and `api.repositories` contain only the repositories under the current workspace folders.

**Actual (intermittent):** the repository of the folder removed by the last swap stays open and visible in Source Control, still there 60 s later. `Git.log` shows its `Opened repository` line a few ms after the folder was removed.

### Suggested fix

After the last await in `openRepository`, just before `this.open(repository)`, skip the open if `repositoryRoot` is no longer inside any workspace folder, unless it was opened explicitly (`openIfParent`/`openIfClosed`, or for an open editor). Another option is a per-path generation token that `onDidChangeWorkspaceFolders` bumps for removed folders, so that in-flight opens for them are dropped.

### Environment

- VS Code 1.142.0 (`6d62b1d5dace93a33b3a6d3eca1317bd5e15bcb1`, x64)
- Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26300
- Built-in `vscode.git` only (`--disable-extensions`)

**Environment:** VS Code 1.142.0 (commit `6d62b1d5dace93a33b3a6d3eca1317bd5e15bcb1`) desktop on Windows, connected through Remote Tunnels to a Linux host. Chat sessions use the Copilot harness, whose agent host runs on the remote host. `chat.customizations.marketplace.enabled` is on, so Customizations shows its Discover page.

**Steps to reproduce**

1. In a remote or tunnel window, register any plugin marketplace, for example in `.github/copilot/settings.json`:

   ```json
   {
     "extraKnownMarketplaces": {
       "example": {
         "source": { "source": "git", "url": "https://<host>/<path-to-repo>", "ref": "main" }
       }
     }
   }
   ```

2. Focus a Copilot harness chat, run **Chat: Open Customizations**, and browse the `example` marketplace on the Discover page.
3. Select **Install** on one of its plugins, for example `my-plugin`.

**Expected:** The plugin installs and is available in new Copilot harness sessions.

**Actual:** The install fails with "Could not install my-plugin. The selected agent does not support SDK plugin installation."

**Analysis**

- Opening Customizations from a Copilot harness chat makes the Copilot harness the active harness. `CustomizationMarketplaceInstallService.install()` then routes the install to that harness's `AgentHostCustomizationMarketplaceInstallProvider`.
- For `configuredPlugin` and `providerPlugin` resources, the provider calls the optional `IAgentHostService.installPlugin`, documented as "Install a plugin through its local Agent Host provider". Only `LocalAgentHostServiceClient` implements it, through `IAgentHostManagementService.installPlugin`, a "local-only bridge". In remote windows the service is `EditorRemoteAgentHostServiceClient`, which doesn't implement it, so the provider throws.
- VS Code's own installer, `IPluginInstallService.installPlugin()`, works in the same window and delivers the plugin to Copilot harness sessions. The Extensions view and the Customizations plugin list use it while `chat.customizations.marketplace.enabled` is off, which is the workaround.
- On `main`, the provider still throws this error when `installPlugin` is missing, and `EditorRemoteAgentHostServiceClient` still doesn't implement it.

**Suggested fix:** In remote windows, fall back to `IPluginInstallService.installPlugin()` for `configuredPlugin` resources, or implement `installPlugin` in the remote agent host client. At a minimum, don't offer **Install** when the active harness can't install.

**Related:** #338975 (moved Copilot plugin installation to the Copilot SDK; its acceptance criteria include preserving remote context).
